---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.idea;

const lines = [10, 5];
const off = 20;
const cut = (x: number) => (x * (100 - off)) / 100;
const v1 = lines.map((x, i) => (i === 0 ? cut(x) : x));
const v2 = lines.map(cut);
const sum = (xs: number[]) => xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
const usd = (n: number) => `$${n}`;
const runs = [
  { code: "v1, discounts only the first line", parts: v1 },
  { code: "v2, discounts both lines", parts: v2 },
];

const first = [6, 10];
const corrected = [8, 10];
const avg = (xs: number[]) => sum(xs) / xs.length;
const states = [
  { id: "kept", label: "Both exports kept", held: [first, corrected] },
  { id: "over", label: "First export overwritten", held: [corrected] },
];
const sameAvg = Array.from({ length: 3 }, (_, i) => [first[0] + i, first[1] - i]).filter(([a, b]) => a <= b);

const saved = "A blue moon";
const reported = 2;
const words = (s: string) => s.split(/\s+/).length;

const checks = [
  ["Only the total", `If the shop had saved only the ${usd(sum(v1))} total, why would the corrected code need the original receipt to calculate the right total?`],
  [`The old ${avg(first)}`, `Why can the teacher’s old average of ${avg(first)} survive even though replay can’t recover the original scores?`],
  ["Two word counts", "Two word counts disagree for the same saved draft. What would you check in their records to find out why?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("idea")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's raw-and-derived study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn={`In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 2 and ${card.returnTask.replace("Then come back and ", "")}`}
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">Raw data, derived data and replay</h2>
  <p><strong>Raw data</strong> is what your system receives from a source. Save an exact copy before your processing code reads it, so a parsing or cleaning bug can’t change the only copy. <strong>Derived data</strong> is what your code calculates from that copy. You can delete and rebuild it if you keep the raw inputs, the code version, and the settings that produced it.</p>
  <p>A <strong>replay</strong> runs the calculation again on saved inputs. Use the original code and settings to reproduce an old result, or use corrected code to regenerate it.</p>
  <p class="pr-pull">Each output should carry a record of its exact input versions, code version, and settings so you can tell which run produced it.</p>

  <h2>1. Retail receipts: a replay fixes the total</h2>
  <p>A shop receives receipt <code>R17</code> with two line amounts, <code>{usd(lines[0])}</code> and <code>{usd(lines[1])}</code>. It saves the receipt exactly as received. The discounted total is derived.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">Two derived totals and their records</p>
    <div class="pa-prov">
      {runs.map((r) => (
        <div>
          <p class="pa-prov-head"><b>Derived total</b><strong>{usd(sum(r.parts))}</strong></p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Saved input</dt><dd><code>R17</code>, revision 1</dd></div>
            <div class="is-diff"><dt>Code</dt><dd>{r.code}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Setting</dt><dd>{off}% off both lines</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="pa-prov-sum">{r.parts.map(usd).join(" + ")} = {usd(sum(r.parts))}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>Same input, same setting. Only the code line differs between the two records. An exact rebuild can also depend on more than these three lines, such as library versions or the date a run used.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Version 1 calculated <code>{v1.map(usd).join(" + ")} = {usd(sum(v1))}</code>. Version 2 calculated <code>{v2.map(usd).join(" + ")} = {usd(sum(v2))}</code>. The records show that the input and settings were identical, so the code change explains the difference. Since both line amounts survived in the archive, the shop can delete the incorrect total and rebuild it with v2.</p>

  <h2>2. Classroom grades: an overwrite loses the earlier scores</h2>
  <p>A teacher exports two quiz scores, <code>{first[0]}</code> and <code>{first[1]}</code>. With equal weights, the derived average is <code>({first.join(" + ")}) ÷ {first.length} = {avg(first)}</code>.</p>
  <p>Later the teacher corrects the first score to <code>{corrected[0]}</code>. The new export contains <code>{corrected[0]}</code> and <code>{corrected[1]}</code>, and the average becomes <code>({corrected.join(" + ")}) ÷ {corrected.length} = {avg(corrected)}</code>. If the import overwrites the archived first export, replay can still produce <code>{avg(corrected)}</code>, but it can’t recover the original scores. Even if the old average of <code>{avg(first)}</code> survives, <code>{sameAvg[1][0]}</code> and <code>{sameAvg[1][1]}</code> would also average to <code>{avg(first)}</code>. Keeping both raw exports under separate revision IDs would let you reproduce either result.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-water" aria-live="polite">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>What the archive holds</legend>
      {states.map((st, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-raw-grades" value={st.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{st.label}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {states.map((st) => (
      <div class="pa-water-view" data-water={st.id}>
        <ul class="pa-rows">
          {[first, corrected].map((ex, i) => {
            const kept = st.held.includes(ex);
            return <li class={kept ? "" : "is-gone"}><span>Export {i + 1}: {kept ? ex.join(" and ") : "overwritten"}</span><b>{kept ? `replays to ${avg(ex)}` : "can't replay"}</b></li>;
          })}
        </ul>
        <p class="pa-water-say">{st.id === "kept"
          ? "Either average can be reproduced from its own export."
          : `The old average of ${avg(first)} may survive as a number, but each of these score pairs gives it:`}</p>
        {st.id === "over" && <ul class="pa-pairs">{sameAvg.map((p) => <li><code>{p.join(" and ")}</code></li>)}</ul>}
      </div>
    ))}
    <figcaption>An average of {avg(first)} leaves several possible score pairs, so only the saved export says which pair it was.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>3. Publishing: a replay can only use the draft that was saved</h2>
  <p>An editor wants to count the words in each draft of a headline. The writer says the first draft had {["no", "one", "two", "three"][reported]} words, but only the later draft, <code>{saved}</code>, was saved. Replaying the word counter can produce <code>{words(saved)}</code> for the saved draft. It can’t recover the earlier wording because that version never entered the archive. The writer’s recollection can supply a reported count of <code>{reported}</code>, but there’s no saved first draft to run the counter on.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <p class="pr-label">What the word counter can run on</p>
    <ul class="pa-rows">
      <li class="is-gone"><span>First draft · never saved</span><b>reported {reported}, not counted</b></li>
      <li class="is-fix"><span>Later draft · <code>{saved}</code></span><b>counted {words(saved)}</b></li>
    </ul>
  </figure>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>In Tuesday's story, the raw data is the schedule pages Kick saved on Friday. Monday's table and Tuesday's table are both derived: each is a calculation over those pages, the parser code and its config. The tidy rule fix is the receipt's: one code change, replayed over the same saved input.</p>
    <p>Dunmore's page is the reply's unsaved first draft, with no writer to recall it: the site never answered on Friday, so there is no copy for a replay to read. Its results still come from the visitors' saved pages, as they did on Monday.</p>
    <p>For the research team's reviewer question, sort its four kept things the reply's way: which is the raw input, which is code, and which are derived. None of the four is the record the reply asks every output to carry, so ask what that leaves the reviewer without.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>4. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-prov { display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-prov { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .pa-prov > div { padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-prov-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .pa-prov-head b { color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .pa-prov-head strong { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.4rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-prov dl { display: grid; gap: 0.25rem; margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .pa-prov dl div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6rem 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 6px; }
  .pa-prov dt { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-prov dd { margin: 0; }
  .pa-prov .is-diff { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, transparent); font-weight: 600; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-prov-sum { margin: 0.45rem 0 0; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.85rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-water .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-water-view { display: none; }
  .pa-water:has([value="kept"]:checked) [data-water="kept"],
  .pa-water:has([value="over"]:checked) [data-water="over"] { display: block; }
  .pa-rows { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-rows li { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 0.3rem 0.6rem; padding: 0.4rem 0.65rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  .pa-rows li b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-rows li.is-fix { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--pr-green); }
  .pa-rows li.is-gone { border: 1.5px dashed var(--pr-red); background: transparent; color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-rows li.is-gone b { color: var(--tl-muted); font-weight: 600; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-water-say { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-pairs { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem; margin: 0.45rem 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-pairs li { padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 999px; font-size: 0.85rem; }
</style>
